# The inline grammar. The fragment token is implemented client-first: the scanner, the renderer,
# the annotate flow (inline create/file/unfile with byte-exact unwrap). Engine-side virtual
# members (query/doctor visibility of inline fragments) are still to come.
The grammar
description: five constructs, atomic tokens, soft breaks - and the fragment token
- >
  Five constructs, and no others. Everything else passes through verbatim.
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  header:
    - Syntax
    - Meaning
      width: 2
  - ['`**bold**`, `__bold__`', strong]
  - ['`*italic*`, `_italic_`', emphasis]
  - ['`~~strike~~`', 'struck through']
  - ['`` `code` ``', 'code span - **atomic**']
  - ['`$$...$$`', 'math, typeset with KaTeX - **atomic**, may span lines']
  - ['`[value](fields)`', 'the **fragment token** - an inline omni; a keyless pointer field makes it a hyperlink']
- >
  **Atomic** means the contents are never re-interpreted as markup: a `*` inside `` `code` `` or
  `$$...$$` is a literal asterisk, not emphasis. Bold is matched before italic, so `**x**` is one
  strong run rather than two emphases. Emphasis **may wrap an atomic token** - `` **`code`** `` is
  a bold code span, `*a `b` c*` an italic run with a code span inside - while a marker *inside*
  the token still can never open or close emphasis. A **fragment token** is an emphasis
  **boundary**: a marker pair spanning one stays literal text; the token styles its own value
  (`[*a*](b)`).
- >
  An **intra-word `_` is a literal character**, never an emphasis marker - Markdown's rule, kept
  for the same reason: technical prose is full of `snake_case_ids`, and an unbackticked
  `unquoted_scalar_appending` must not italicize its middle. Only a `_` at a word boundary opens
  or closes emphasis (`__` likewise). `*` keeps intra-word emphasis - identifiers don't use it.
- The fragment token
  - >
    `[...](...)` is **flow syntax for an omni**: the `[]` part holds a node's **self-value** (and
    optional `&` bookmarks), the `()` part its **fields**, spelled `key: value` and
    comma-separated. In prose the omni it spells is a **text fragment** - a member of the chunk's
    `.yo: fragments:` ([fragments](*::annotations:fragments)) whose self-value is the token's
    visible text. A **keyless pointer or URL field is the fragment's link**, so a token whose
    parens hold only that is an ordinary hyperlink; empty parens (`[once to scan]()`) define a
    fragment with no fields. A bare `[text]` without parens stays plain prose, as do bare
    `{...}`/`(...)` - marklower claims **only the full token**.
  - 'One token, and the member it means - the link rides as a keyless pointer entry:'
  - !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
    reads the file twice: [once to scan](*::annotations:fragments), once to build
  - !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
    'reads the file twice: once to scan, once to build'
    .yo: fragments:
      - once to scan
        type: text
        prefix: 'reads the file twice: '
        suffix: ', once to build'
        - *:: annotations: fragments
  - >
    Inside `[]` the **yamlover scalar rules** apply - quote the value when it contains a colon. A
    bookmark ladder is self-delimiting when it ends in `-` (an [onto](*::annotations)
    membership: `[&:: yamlover: ontos: colors: yellow: -: once to scan]()` - the push, then the
    text) and must be the quoted spelling otherwise (`[&': put: me: here' once to scan]()`),
    exactly the [bookmarks](*::language:pointers:bookmarks) placement law.
  - >
    The token is marklower's **normalized** spelling of a text fragment; the explicit
    `.yo: fragments:` member is the same fragment authored from the other end - legal but
    denormalized, the forward/reverse pair of bookmarks all over again. Folding an explicit
    member into the text is possible only where its quote resolves uniquely and crosses no other
    token; elsewhere the explicit form stays. Formats other than marklower have no token to fold
    into - there the explicit member is the only spelling. The fragment's `prefix`/`suffix`
    (and any `start`/`end`) are **derived from the token's own position**, never written in it.
- >
  There is **no embed syntax** - marklower delegates structure to yamlover, and embedding *is*
  structure: a chapter body element (see [embeds](*::documents:marklower:embeds)). A `*` before a
  token's bracket is plain text: `*[a](b)*` is a literal asterisk pair around a token (a token is
  an emphasis boundary - italicize the value instead), a bare `*[a](b)` a literal asterisk
  followed by a token. The `*` that matters lives INSIDE the parens, as a
  pointer sigil ([link targets](*::documents:marklower:link-targets)).
- >
  Marklower is **not a Markdown subset**, and does not try to be. `![](...)` is not image syntax
  here, a link target is a yamlover pointer expression, which Markdown has no word for - and
  `[..](..)` says more here than Markdown's link ever did.
- Line breaks
  - >
    A **single newline is a soft break**: the hard-wrapped lines of a block scalar are a *source*
    courtesy, so they rejoin into one flowing run and the rendered prose wraps at the reader's
    chosen width instead of the authored column - Markdown's rule, kept for the same reason. The
    join crosses an inline token (`text\n$$x$$` is one sentence), and emphasis may span it. A
    **blank line stays**: it renders as the vertical gap the author drew.
